# Python Development Rules

## Code Style
- Use Python 3.11+ features
- Follow PEP 8, PEP 257 (docstrings)
- Use type hints everywhere
- Maximum line length: 100 characters
- Use double quotes for strings
- Use pathlib instead of os.path

## Type Hints
- All functions must have type hints
- Use modern type hints (list[str] instead of List[str])
- Use typing.Protocol for structural subtyping
- Use typing.Final for constants

## Error Handling
- Use specific exceptions, not bare except
- Always include error messages
- Use contextlib for resource management

## Testing
- Write tests for all new functions
- Use pytest fixtures
- Aim for 80%+ code coverage
- Use parametrize for multiple test cases

## Documentation
- All modules, classes, and functions need docstrings
- Use Google-style docstrings
- Include type information in docstrings
- Document exceptions raised

## Security
- No hardcoded secrets
- Use environment variables for configuration
- Validate all external inputs
- Use bandit security checks

## Imports
- Standard library first
- Third-party second
- Local imports last
- Use absolute imports

## Tools
- Run ruff check and format on save
- Run mypy before committing
- Run pytest before pushing
- Use pre-commit hooks

## Commit Messages
- Use conventional commits format
- Types: feat, fix, docs, style, refactor, test, chore
- Example: "feat: add user authentication"
